> (1) Why do armor classes range from 10 (worst) to
> -40 (best), rather than from 0 (worst) to 50
> (best)?  The latter would be much more
> straightforward.

It's been 20 years, so I may get this wrong, but as
I remember, having armor class run backwards makes a
certain calculation easier to do in the Dungeon
Master's head, saving a lot of time fumbling around
with tables to look up results.

An adventurer's attack with a particular weapon,
it's enchantment, dexterity, strength(?), any
bonuses, and whatever, can be combined into a single
statitistic, THAC0 ("to hit armor class zero") which
can be applied to battling _any_monster. It is the
number the adventurer's player must meet or exceed
on an attack roll of the dice to get a successful
"hit" when taking a blow at the monster, _on the
presumption_ that the monster's armor class is zero.

Now, for the monster actually in front of the
adventurer, say it has armor classes, for two other
examples than zero, four, or minus six.  You take
the roll (of a twenty sided die) you actually get,
add the armor class, say four, to a roll of say ten,
and if the adventurer's THAC0 is no more than "14",
the hit was successful.

Similarly, if the monster's AC was -6, you add that
to the roll of 10, giving 4, and the adventurer gets
a hit only if his/her THAC0 is 4 or less (a pretty
awesome fighter stat).

This calculation would be a bunch more cumbersome if
AC got better as it got bigger.

